<BaseLayout title="PostgreSQL Plan Regression — DatabaseDeveloper" description="Same SQL. Different plan. Very different production behavior. Practical evidence to collect, common wrong assumptions and when a deeper investigation is justified.">
  <PageHero eyebrow="Problem guide" title="PostgreSQL Plan Regression" description="Same SQL. Different plan. Very different production behavior." />
  <section><div class="container grid three">
    <div class="card"><h3>Cardinality</h3><p>Compare estimated vs actual rows and data distribution changes.</p></div>
<div class="card"><h3>Prepared statements</h3><p>Generic/custom plan behavior can create parameter-sensitive regressions.</p></div>
<div class="card"><h3>Change timeline</h3><p>Tie plan changes back to deployment, statistics, data growth, version or configuration changes.</p></div>
  </div></section>
  <section><div class="container prose">
    <h2>Investigation principle</h2>
    <blockquote>Do not force a plan before understanding why the optimizer changed its choice.</blockquote>
    <h2>Common wrong assumptions</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>“The SQL didn’t change, so the plan didn’t.”</strong> Plans depend on statistics, parameter values, data volume, settings and version. Any of them can change while the SQL text stays the same.</li>
      <li><strong>“Running ANALYZE fixed it, so we’re done.”</strong> Then find out why autoanalyze didn’t keep up. Large tables can wait a long time between automatic analyzes, and correlated columns may need extended statistics (<code>CREATE STATISTICS</code>).</li>
      <li><strong>“EXPLAIN in psql shows what the app gets.”</strong> The application may use prepared statements, generic plans or different session settings. The <a href="/sample-report">sample report</a> shows a case where psql looks fine and the application doesn’t.</li>
      <li><strong>“Just force the old plan.”</strong> Disabling planner node types or pinning plans hides the cause. It also breaks again when the data shifts.</li>
    </ul>
    <h2>First queries to run</h2>
    <p>Read-only. Run them during the problem window if you can; most of these views show current state, not history.</p>
<pre><code>-- Tables with the most changes since their last analyze
SELECT relname,
       n_live_tup,
       n_mod_since_analyze,
       last_analyze,
       last_autoanalyze
FROM pg_stat_user_tables
ORDER BY n_mod_since_analyze DESC
LIMIT 10;

-- Then, in a safe environment, compare estimated vs actual rows on each plan node:
EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS) &lt;the regressed query with representative parameters&gt;;

-- To capture the plan production actually ran, use auto_explain:
--   auto_explain.log_min_duration = '500ms'  (session_preload_libraries or shared_preload_libraries)</code></pre>
    <h2>When a deeper investigation is justified</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>The regression comes and goes, which points at parameter values, statistics timing or plan caching.</li>
      <li>Estimates are off by orders of magnitude and the cause isn’t obvious from one plan.</li>
      <li>The candidate fixes (statistics, an index, a query rewrite or plan-cache settings) each carry risk, and someone has to pick one.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>See the <a href="/case-studies/lab-generic-plan-regression">measured generic-plan regression lab</a> for a complete change, evidence and response sequence.</p>
  </div></section>
  <section><div class="container"><CTA /></div></section>
</BaseLayout>
